          <text x="525" y="186" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-text-secondary)">fills gaps, binds parties</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div class="svg-caption">Higher sources override lower ones when they conflict. Common law countries treat case law as binding through precedent. Civil law countries give it persuasive weight. All modern systems are statute-heavy, but constitutional supremacy is the usual cap.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="period-chart">
      <div class="chart-title">Long arc of legal institutions, 1215-2026</div>
      <div class="pc-inner">
        <div class="pc-axis" id="lawTimelineAxis"></div>
        <div id="lawTimelineRows"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-note">Bars are institutional influence periods, not exact birth and death dates. Magna Carta mattered in 1215, but its myth and legal afterlife became much larger centuries later.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="mini-buttons" id="traditionButtons">
      <button type="button" data-key="common">Common law</button>
      <button type="button" data-key="civil">Civil law</button>
      <button type="button" data-key="religious">Religious law</button>
      <button type="button" data-key="mixed">Mixed systems</button>
      <button type="button" data-key="customary">Customary law</button>
    </div>
    <div class="explorer-panel" id="traditionDisplay"></div>

    <div class="svg-card">
      <div class="chart-title">How a dispute becomes a legal decision</div>
